#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120715} :activity/id #uuid "80511d12-b7f3-5570-8b75-694c2aa3ad46" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050830 :topic/id #uuid "6a093d52-40f2-54b8-8982-db428e58bab5" :topic/math-academy-id 1807} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120715 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121050 :tutorial/content "If we are given an equation of a plane using the dot product, we can always obtain an equation in terms of $x,$ $y,$ and $z.$ For example, suppose we have the plane that passes through the point $P(-7,5,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 3,-6,5 \\rangle.$\n\nFirst, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -7,5,-2 \\rangle,$ and we can write down the equation of the plane using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-6,5⟩ & =⟨-7,5,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-6,5⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-6,5⟩ & =-61\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-6,5⟩ & =-61 \\\\ 3x-6y+5z & =-61 \\\\ 3x-6y+5z+61 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis final equation is called the **Cartesian equation of the plane**.\n\n![](images/77/77f18981dda500889655225eabbec730b7b295e6b16cec1700d56c2e27e0ac91.png)\n\nNote that in the equation \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}x+({\\color{blue}-6})y+{\\color{blue}5}z+61 = 0,\n$$\n\nthe coefficients next to $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ are the coordinates of the normal vector \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle {\\color{blue}3},{\\color{blue}-6},{\\color{blue}5} \\rangle\n$$\n\n." :tutorial/id #uuid "81332607-6152-5770-b33d-8b80c630fc8d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3256 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c247ca81-f4a5-53d1-8429-f69593877343" :step/math-academy-id 17922 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120716}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120716 :ste